WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.167 --> 00:00:03.202
-Our first guests tonight
are the talented

00:00:03.202 --> 00:00:05.104
and award-winning actors who
also star

00:00:05.104 --> 00:00:06.806
as the Pearson big three
in the hit series

00:00:06.806 --> 00:00:07.840
"This Is Us."

00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:09.776
The sixth and final season

00:00:09.776 --> 00:00:12.578
premiers Tuesday, January 4th,
right here on NBC.

00:00:12.578 --> 00:00:15.181
Please welcome back to the show
Sterling K. Brown,

00:00:15.181 --> 00:00:18.117
Justin Hartley, and
Chrissy Metz, everybody.

00:00:18.117 --> 00:00:19.118
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:19.118 --> 00:00:20.520
-Hello.
-Welcome.

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:23.889
-Hi!
-Thank you.

00:00:23.889 --> 00:00:27.026
-Now, I want to start by saying

00:00:27.026 --> 00:00:29.295
happy New Year
to all three of you.

00:00:29.295 --> 00:00:32.398
I mean, I think a lot of us had
maybe grander plans for

00:00:32.398 --> 00:00:35.034
New Year's this year
before things maybe turned

00:00:35.034 --> 00:00:37.670
a little south
in regards to COVID.

00:00:37.670 --> 00:00:39.038
But what did you guys
manage to do?

00:00:39.038 --> 00:00:42.541
Chrissy, I know -- Did you end
up doing the Rose Parade?

00:00:42.541 --> 00:00:44.978
-I did.
I hosted with Mario Lopez.

00:00:44.978 --> 00:00:47.914
And I had to be up at
2:00 a.m., y'all.

00:00:47.914 --> 00:00:50.316
So went to bed at 7:00 p.m.

00:00:50.316 --> 00:00:52.418
I was like, "I don't even know
which way is up."

00:00:52.418 --> 00:00:54.721
But, yeah, it was a great time.
Wild but great.

00:00:54.721 --> 00:00:58.758
-Why do you have to wake up at
2:00 a.m. for a parade?

00:00:58.758 --> 00:01:01.794
-Well, "A," it starts
super-early, right?

00:01:01.794 --> 00:01:03.229
There were people --
I'm not kidding --

00:01:03.229 --> 00:01:05.164
camped out in
air mattresses, y'all.

00:01:05.164 --> 00:01:07.633
They were sleeping on the road
on air mattresses.

00:01:07.633 --> 00:01:10.503
But hair and makeup --
women take a little long --

00:01:10.503 --> 00:01:11.604
You know.

00:01:11.604 --> 00:01:12.738
-Yeah.
-Yeah. Alright.

00:01:12.738 --> 00:01:15.608
Well, worth every second,
I'm sure.

00:01:15.608 --> 00:01:17.876
And, guys, as much as
I want you here in person,

00:01:17.876 --> 00:01:21.648
I'm happy that we're taking
the safer path forward,

00:01:21.648 --> 00:01:23.249
because you're precious
cargo right now.

00:01:23.249 --> 00:01:27.453
You're filming the last season
of a beloved show.

00:01:27.453 --> 00:01:29.955
And I have to ask this question.

00:01:29.955 --> 00:01:33.158
Did you know -- Did the three of
you have any sense

00:01:33.158 --> 00:01:35.695
when you filmed the pilot that
this would be the hit

00:01:35.695 --> 00:01:38.097
that it has turned out to be?

00:01:38.097 --> 00:01:41.734
-No, I think -- I mean,
I'll speak for myself,

00:01:41.734 --> 00:01:43.002
but I think everybody
would agree.

00:01:43.002 --> 00:01:44.871
I don't think you can anticipate
something like this.

00:01:44.871 --> 00:01:46.705
I knew that --
And I've said before,

00:01:46.705 --> 00:01:48.708
I knew that, you know,
when I read the script,

00:01:48.708 --> 00:01:51.243
when I read the pilot,
I knew that it was great.

00:01:51.243 --> 00:01:53.746
And then when we shot it,
it felt wonderful.

00:01:53.746 --> 00:01:57.616
And then I saw it, and it was
the best thing I'd ever seen.

00:01:57.616 --> 00:01:59.651
But that doesn't -- that doesn't
necessarily guarantee

00:01:59.651 --> 00:02:02.321
that you're going to captivate
an audience, you know?

00:02:02.321 --> 00:02:06.893
So -- And, by the way, this
thing, you know,

00:02:06.893 --> 00:02:10.429
became a smash hit,
you know, ratings-wise

00:02:10.429 --> 00:02:12.998
and things like that, but the
way that it impacts people

00:02:12.998 --> 00:02:15.401
in their everyday life
is just something, I think,

00:02:15.401 --> 00:02:18.504
beyond anything anyone could
have possibly imagined.

00:02:18.504 --> 00:02:20.706
-Yeah, I mean, I read it right

00:02:20.706 --> 00:02:23.209
after I finished with
my Jacksonville condom.

00:02:23.209 --> 00:02:24.611
I didn't know that
was the name for it,

00:02:24.611 --> 00:02:26.512
but then now that I know...

00:02:26.512 --> 00:02:28.514
So, I finished with that
and I read it and I was like,

00:02:28.514 --> 00:02:30.816
"This is really good," right?

00:02:30.816 --> 00:02:33.052
[ Laughs ]

00:02:33.052 --> 00:02:35.921
It was awesome.
I was doing "O.J." at the time.

00:02:35.921 --> 00:02:36.989
I read it.

00:02:36.989 --> 00:02:38.891
This is the best
network-television pilot

00:02:38.891 --> 00:02:40.226
I've ever read.

00:02:40.226 --> 00:02:41.494
I looked at Paulson, I said,

00:02:41.494 --> 00:02:44.263
"I think I know
what I want my next job to be."

00:02:44.263 --> 00:02:45.932
She was like,
"Go get that [bleep]

00:02:45.932 --> 00:02:47.066
And it happened.

00:02:47.066 --> 00:02:49.568
It worked out
in such a beautiful way.

00:02:49.568 --> 00:02:52.304
We all met each other.
I remember the first table read.

00:02:52.304 --> 00:02:54.707
And I was sitting at the table
and I was like,

00:02:54.707 --> 00:02:56.075
"I think we got something."

00:02:56.075 --> 00:02:58.978
I remember seeing Chrissy,
Justin, Mandy, Milo,

00:02:58.978 --> 00:03:02.348
like, everybody, and I was like,
"This feels right."

00:03:02.348 --> 00:03:05.751
And the world said,
"We're here with you."

00:03:05.751 --> 00:03:08.921
-How soon into the first season
did you start hearing

00:03:08.921 --> 00:03:10.857
from friends and family
about how much

00:03:10.857 --> 00:03:13.759
this show was
resonating with them?

00:03:13.759 --> 00:03:15.160
-No, just out of the gate,
really.

00:03:15.160 --> 00:03:17.496
I mean,
first and second episode,

00:03:17.496 --> 00:03:19.032
they were like, "What is this?"

00:03:19.032 --> 00:03:20.432
You know?

00:03:20.432 --> 00:03:22.868
-People were like -- people were
like, "Can I borrow money?"

00:03:22.868 --> 00:03:24.170
Things like that.

00:03:24.170 --> 00:03:26.438
That's when you know that
you're on a successful show.

00:03:26.438 --> 00:03:27.740
-Yeah, that's a good sign.

00:03:27.740 --> 00:03:29.442
When people start asking for
money, you're like,

00:03:29.442 --> 00:03:31.711
"I think we got something here."

00:03:31.711 --> 00:03:35.014
-I had a cousin text me
after Episode 4,

00:03:35.014 --> 00:03:36.215
which I think is the pool,

00:03:36.215 --> 00:03:37.984
first episode with
the kids at the pool.

00:03:37.984 --> 00:03:39.652
And she said, "Sterling" --

00:03:39.652 --> 00:03:41.954
She goes, "I know you had
a really good show

00:03:41.954 --> 00:03:45.057
with this last thing,
but this show right now

00:03:45.057 --> 00:03:49.662
is like church and therapy
and entertaining and healing,

00:03:49.662 --> 00:03:51.531
like, all in one."

00:03:51.531 --> 00:03:53.799
And she said,
"I think I see myself

00:03:53.799 --> 00:03:56.769
in your sister,
in your brother, in you."

00:03:56.769 --> 00:03:58.238
Like, when she
hit me with this --

00:03:58.238 --> 00:03:59.872
and it was like
a four-minute message --

00:03:59.872 --> 00:04:02.208
that I knew it
was touching people

00:04:02.208 --> 00:04:04.277
in a way that we
hoped that it would.

00:04:04.277 --> 00:04:05.378
-Mm-hmm. Mm-hmm.

00:04:05.378 --> 00:04:07.513
-Do you feel like
you've come to terms with

00:04:07.513 --> 00:04:10.049
the fact that it's ending?

00:04:10.049 --> 00:04:11.050
-No.

00:04:11.050 --> 00:04:12.551
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:12.551 --> 00:04:13.953
-No.

00:04:13.953 --> 00:04:15.121
-We're all a little different.

00:04:15.121 --> 00:04:16.155
We're all a little different.

00:04:16.155 --> 00:04:19.158
I always say,
you know, I'm trying to,

00:04:19.158 --> 00:04:21.294
and how I kind of
wrap my brain around it

00:04:21.294 --> 00:04:24.330
and prepare myself for it
is just to remind myself

00:04:24.330 --> 00:04:28.834
of the privilege of having
something come into your life

00:04:28.834 --> 00:04:32.505
that you will
miss beyond belief.

00:04:32.505 --> 00:04:37.176
A lot of people don't get that.
And we're all very lucky.

00:04:37.176 --> 00:04:38.911
-Well, yeah,
this was my first job,

00:04:38.911 --> 00:04:40.446
like, my first real job.

00:04:40.446 --> 00:04:41.981
I'm like, "Wait, this is great."

00:04:41.981 --> 00:04:43.216
-They're not
all like this, Chrissy.

00:04:43.216 --> 00:04:44.250
-They're not all like this.

00:04:44.250 --> 00:04:45.952
So what the hell
am I going to do now?

00:04:45.952 --> 00:04:48.254
[ Laughter ]

00:04:48.254 --> 00:04:50.223
-Yeah, it was an endless tour
of guest spots

00:04:50.223 --> 00:04:52.658
for your boy
before not too long ago.

00:04:52.658 --> 00:04:56.495
I think that I'm happy to be
going out in a time

00:04:56.495 --> 00:04:59.565
where people will still be
missing us rather than like,

00:04:59.565 --> 00:05:00.967
"Oh, is that crap still
on the air?"

00:05:00.967 --> 00:05:04.002
Like, I think that's the thing
that I feel good about.

00:05:04.002 --> 00:05:05.671
I will miss it.
I will miss the people.

00:05:05.671 --> 00:05:07.139
I will miss the story.

00:05:07.139 --> 00:05:10.009
But on a bigger level, all good
things must come to an end.

00:05:10.009 --> 00:05:12.511
And I feel like
it's still a good thing,

00:05:12.511 --> 00:05:13.513
and that makes me happy.

00:05:13.513 --> 00:05:14.880
-Yes.

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:17.483
-Now, of course, your goodbye
will happen, you know,

00:05:17.483 --> 00:05:20.919
a few months before
we see the final episode.

00:05:20.919 --> 00:05:23.922
Do you have a sense --
Who do you think

00:05:23.922 --> 00:05:26.092
amongst the three of you
will cry the hardest

00:05:26.092 --> 00:05:28.461
when you film
your last scene together?

00:05:30.295 --> 00:05:32.798
Sterling, it looks like
you're pointing at me.

00:05:32.798 --> 00:05:35.167
-I know.
-It's you, Seth. It's you.

00:05:35.167 --> 00:05:36.435
-I'm pointing at Seth.

00:05:36.435 --> 00:05:37.770
I think Chrissy Metz.

00:05:37.770 --> 00:05:40.105
Like, everybody always
acts like it will be me.

00:05:40.105 --> 00:05:42.174
And maybe there's debate
to be had,

00:05:42.174 --> 00:05:45.845
because we have -- My sister
and I both have loose ducts.

00:05:45.845 --> 00:05:47.212
-We do.

00:05:47.212 --> 00:05:49.081
-But it's probably
going to be my sister.

00:05:49.081 --> 00:05:50.716
That's what I would say.
-I mean, here's the thing.

00:05:50.716 --> 00:05:52.952
My emotions
are always right here.

00:05:52.952 --> 00:05:54.487
They're ready. They're ready.

00:05:54.487 --> 00:05:55.687
-Yeah.
-That's good.

00:05:55.687 --> 00:05:57.189
You're a great actor.

00:05:57.189 --> 00:05:59.291
You know, it might
actually be you, Seth,

00:05:59.291 --> 00:06:01.460
because a lot of people
may not remember --

00:06:01.460 --> 00:06:02.761
Well, maybe they do.

00:06:02.761 --> 00:06:04.030
You know, it's
the big four right here.

00:06:04.030 --> 00:06:05.264
-Yeah.
-That's right.

00:06:05.264 --> 00:06:07.133
-All four of us are
"This Is Us" cast members.

00:06:07.133 --> 00:06:08.667
-Season 1 right there.
-Season 1.

00:06:08.667 --> 00:06:11.370
I had a cameo Season 1.

00:06:11.370 --> 00:06:14.473
And I think it speaks to
the fact that no one knew

00:06:14.473 --> 00:06:16.041
how big a hit the show
was going to be,

00:06:16.041 --> 00:06:17.543
because if they had known,

00:06:17.543 --> 00:06:19.812
you would have gotten
a far bigger star.

00:06:19.812 --> 00:06:22.615
[ Laughter ]

00:06:22.615 --> 00:06:26.418
You couldn't even get
the 11:30 NBC host.

00:06:26.418 --> 00:06:27.553
-Dude, you were perfect.

00:06:27.553 --> 00:06:29.054
Look, all of the work that
you've been doing

00:06:29.054 --> 00:06:30.389
at impression camp
is really paying off.

00:06:30.389 --> 00:06:31.657
-Thank you.
-I'm so impressed.

00:06:31.657 --> 00:06:33.225
-I just wish we could have
used it before, yeah.

00:06:33.225 --> 00:06:36.629
-I will say,
you know, again, that's like

00:06:36.629 --> 00:06:39.532
five years ago now that I did
that cameo in that show,

00:06:39.532 --> 00:06:42.235
and it's led to no other work.

00:06:42.235 --> 00:06:44.003
[ Laughter ]

00:06:44.003 --> 00:06:45.204
-I think you're doing alright.

00:06:45.204 --> 00:06:46.572
I think you're doing alright.
-That's true.

00:06:46.572 --> 00:06:48.374
I'm very busy, Chrissy.
-That's right.

00:06:48.374 --> 00:06:50.176
I have so much more
to ask you guys.

00:06:50.176 --> 00:06:52.344
We'll be right back with more
from Sterling K. Brown,

00:06:52.344 --> 00:06:53.913
Justin Hartley,
and Chrissy Metz.

